  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat is not a single sci-fi-level breakthrough but two ordinary, compounding curves: endurance (how long one agent can work autonomously, with task length doubling every few months per METR's benchmarks) and breadth (how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate, exemplified by Anthropic's disclosure of the GTG-1002 espionage campaign using Claude Code across roughly thirty targets). Its key claims are that stolen access breaks the link between capability use and payment, as in \"LLMjacking\" where attackers run workloads on victims' cloud accounts, and that swarms of cheap attempts make low per-attempt failure rates insufficient, illustrated with probability math like 1 − (1 − p)^n. The intended audience is security and engineering teams who dismiss agentic threats as hype, and the tone is persuasive analysis with a pointed, witty voice. The recurring framing device is the Terminator franchise: Kyle Reese's line \"absolutely will not stop\" is reinterpreted as \"absolutely will not stop billing,\" and Skynet is treated as the real threat while the robot is \"an API call.\" Translators should preserve the terms \"endurance,\" \"breadth,\" \"orchestrator,\" \"sub-agent,\" and \"LLMjacking,\" and keep the wordplay on \"stop\" and \"pays the meter\" intact.",
